e', 'title': 'Episode Name'}\n   - info = {'episode': 'Episode Name', 'title': 'Episode Name'}\n   \n   Then merge_dicts(base, info). merge_dicts semantics (from get_info):\n   \n   ```python\n   def merge_dicts(*dicts):\n       merged = {}\n       for a_dict in dicts:\n           for k, v in a_dict.items():\n               if v is None:\n                   continue\n               if k not in merged or (isinstance(v, compat_str) and v and isinstance(merged[k], compat_str) and (not merged[k])):\n                   merged[k] = v\n       return merged\n   ```\n   \n   So: FIRST dict wins (later values only override if existing merged value is an empty string and new value is non-empty string). So base dict's title (og:title) wins over info's title. And 'episode': 'Episode Name' gets added (not in base).\n   \n   So with real `_json_ld`, result['episode'] == 'Episode Name', result['title'] == 'Test Episode Title' (og:title wins). \n   \n   This is a much better integration test! But it depends on real `_json_ld` behavior \u2014 which I now have the source for, so I can assert precisely. Hmm, but should I? The risk: if the installed youtube_dl version's `_json_ld` differs from what get_info showed... get_info showed the actual installed source, so it's reliable.\n   \n   But wait \u2014 there's a catch with real `_search_json_ld`: it calls `self._parse_json(mobj.group('json_ld'), video_id, fatal=fatal)` where fatal = False (since default != NO_DEFAULT). Fine. And `self._json_ld(json_ld_list, ...)` \u2014 real. OK.\n   \n   Hmm, but for tests 2-6, I want to control the `_search_json_ld` result (e.g., non-empty for test 4). Options:\n   \n   a) Mock `_search_json_ld` in all tests (uniform, controlled).\n   b) Use real `_search_json_ld` everywhere, controlling via webpage content:\n      - Test 1: BreadcrumbList (no @context) \u2192 {} \u2192 fallback \u2192 TVEpisode \u2192 real _json_ld \u2192 info.\n      - Test 2: no JSON-LD script \u2192 json_ld_list empty \u2192 json