n   Wait, but hmm: `_search_json_ld` calls `_json_ld(json_ld_list, ...)` where json_ld_list = [breadcrumb, tvep] (in document order). `_json_ld` iterates: breadcrumb (no @context) skipped; tvep (has @context) \u2192 processed \u2192 info = {'episode': 'Search Episode', 'title': 'Search Episode'}. Returns that. Non-empty \u2192 line 131 skips fallback. result['episode'] == 'Search Episode'. And result['title'] == og:title (base wins in merge). \n   \n   Nice. That's a solid test 4.\n\n10. **parse_duration**: The source shows:\n    - First regex: colon-separated `[[[days:]hours:]mins:]secs[.ms]Z?` \u2014 '153' matches (secs=153) \u2192 153.0. \n    - Second regex (VERBOSE): `(?:P?(?:...years?...)?(?:...months?...)?(?:...weeks?...)?(?:days d)?T)?(?:hours h)?(?:mins m)?(?:secs s)?Z?` \u2014 hmm, does 'PT1H2M3S' match? The pattern: `(?ix)(?:P?(?:[0-9]+\\s*y...)?(?:[0-9]+\\s*m...)?(?:[0-9]+\\s*w...)?(?:[0-9]+\\s*d...)?T)?(?:([0-9]+)\\s*h...)?(?:([0-9]+)\\s*m...)?(?:([0-9]+)(\\.[0-9]+)?\\s*s...)?Z?$`.\n    \n    For 'PT1H2M3S': P? matches 'P'... wait, `P?` then the group of y/m/w/d then `T`. So `P?` matches 'P', then optional y/m/w/d groups (none), then `T` \u2014 matches 'T'. Then hours: `1\\s*h` \u2192 '1H' (case-insensitive) \u2713. mins: `2\\s*m(?:in(?:ute)?s?)?` \u2192 '2M' \u2713. secs: `3\\s*s` \u2192 '3S' \u2713. Then Z? and $ \u2713. So 'PT1H2M3S' \u2192 hours=1, mins=2, secs=3 \u2192 3600+120+3 = 3723.0. \n    \n    Wait, but careful \u2014 the second regex would also try... first regex tried first: 'PT1H2M3S' vs colon pattern \u2014 no match. Second: match \u2192 3723.0. But hmm, one concern: the `m(?:onths?)?` group is unnamed and the days group is named `(?P<days>...)`. For 'PT1H2M3S', the months group `[0-9]+\\s*m(?:onths?)?` \u2014 could it greedily match '2M'?? The structure: `(?:P?(?:(?:[0-9]+\\s*y)?(?:(?:[0-9]+\\s*m(?:onths?)?)?(?:(?:[0-9]+\\s*w)?(?:(?P<days>[0-9]+)\\s*d)?T)?...`. Hmm, the exact nesting from the source:\n    \n    ```\n    (?ix)(?:P?\n            (?:\n                [0-9]+\\s*y(?:ears?)?\\s*\n            )?\n            (?:\n                [0-9]+\\s*m(?:onths?)?\\s*\n            )?\n            (?:\n                [0-9]+\\s*w(?:eeks?)?\\s*\n            )?\n            (?:\n                (?P<days>[0-9]+)\\s*d(?:ays?)?\\s*\n            )?\n            T)?\n            (?:\n                (?P<hours>[0-9]+)\\s*h(?:ours?)?\\s*\n            )?\n            (?:\n                (?P<mins>[0-9]+)\\s*m(?:in(?:ute)?s?)?\\s*\n            )?\n            (?:\n                (?P<secs>[0-9]+)(?P<ms>\\.[0-9]+)?\\s*s(?:ec(?:ond)?s?)?\\s*\n            )?Z?$\n    ```\n    \n    For 'PT1H2M3S': after `P?` consumes 'P', the inner group tries: y? no; months? `[0-9]+\\s*m...` \u2014 next char is 'T', no; weeks? no; days? `[0-9]+d` \u2014 'T' no; then `T` \u2014 matches 'T'. Then hours '1H' \u2713, mins '2M' \u2713, secs '3S' \u2713, $ \u2713. \n    \n    But wait \u2014 could the months group match '2M' if the T group matched differently? The regex engine tries the path above first and it succeeds fully. But hmm, actually there's a subtle issue: `P?` \u2014 what if the engine matches P? as empty and then... 'P' would need to match `[0-9]+\\s*y` etc. \u2014 no. So P? must consume 'P'. Then T consumed. Fine. \u2192 3723.0. \n    \n    Hmm, but actually wait: there's an ambiguity \u2014 'PT1H2M3S': could `(?P<mins>[0-9]+)\\s*m(?:onths?)?` inside the FIRST group match '2M'?? The first group is `(?:P?(?:y)?(?:m)?(?:w)?(?:d)?T)?` \u2014 for months to match '2M', the engine would need '2' right after P/T position... After 'P', next is 'T' \u2014 months needs [0-9]+ \u2192 'T' fails. So no. But what about: `P?` matches empty, then y/m/w/d all fail on 'P', then T fails on 'P' \u2192 whole group matches empty \u2192 then hours `[0-9]+h` on 'PT1H2M3S' \u2192 'P' fails \u2192 whole regex fails \u2192 backtrack \u2192 P? consumes 'P' \u2192 as analyzed \u2192 success. Good: 3723.0.\n    \n    Hmm, but one more: 'PT1H2M3S' \u201